  "worked_solution": "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t\\,i+{sec}^{2}\u2061t\\,j+\\,k \\\\ {r}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,i+{sec}^{2}\u2061\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$  is  $\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k.$",
